    "created_at": "2021-01-21T08:46:31Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-01-21T08:46:31Z",
    "body": "I'm interested in implementing this. It looks like rayon has to be replaced with an async runtime, along with significant async-ification. Rayon does not provide any sort timeout facility (nor is it trying to), while timeouts are comparatively easy to manage in async environments.\r\n\r\nIn my testing, starship usually calls an external process, which takes long time to complete, so with today's rayon-based parallelism, timeouts will usually expire while a thread is blocked, waiting on that subprocess.\r\n\r\nThus, (some of) the module code will need to become async, so each module invocation can be timed out. This does mean that:\r\n* processes may continue in the background after the prompt is displayed, especially the i/o starved ones. These can either be handled on a case-by-case basis, or starship could enumerate its children (by PID) and kill them before exiting.\r\n* there may be some non-async crates that end up blocking a runtime thread. For those individual cases, it's likely sufficient to spawn a dedicated thread, and await the result with a timeout via a channel.\r\n\r\nThis will be a pretty large delta when it comes thru, but I think it will be worth it, since it will provide a basic general fallback for all the current major timing issues. Of course, the complete solution will probably involve some combination of caching, persistence, and maybe a daemon, but that can be added later.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
