    "updated_at": "2021-01-21T11:29:40Z",
    "body": "@alexmaco\r\n\r\nI think #1446 +  #2171 are going to de-facto solve this issue, but regarding async:\r\n\r\n>  processes may continue in the background after the prompt is displayed, especially the i/o starved ones. These can either be handled on a case-by-case basis, or starship could enumerate its children (by PID) and kill them before exiting.\r\n\r\nI think killing might be difficult to implement correctly. The process_control crate docs mention if done incorrectly this kind of thing may kill the wrong process.\r\n\r\n> there may be some non-async crates that end up blocking a runtime thread. For those individual cases, it's likely sufficient to spawn a dedicated thread, and await the result with a timeout via a channel.\r\n\r\nAre there any rayon-like async runtimes that create a lot of worker threads to side step this issue? But so far the only things that I think are slowing the prompt down significantly are external commands and listing files in the current directory which should be easy to async. I guess git status is still slow and non-async until #1446 is fixed.\r\n\r\nRegarding async I'm also a bit worried that the runtimes might have a high initial set-up cost before they get going.\r\n\r\nYou might be able to pick up some code from #922",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
