    "created_at": "2021-01-21T14:19:59Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-01-21T14:21:22Z",
    "body": "@davidkna \r\n\r\n> I think killing might be difficult to implement correctly. The process_control crate docs mention if done incorrectly this kind of thing may kill the wrong process.\r\n\r\nIndeed, it's not a simple problem. It will probably need a pidfd-like mechanism, or an internal handle proxy layer + registry, similar to today's `utils::exec_cmd`.\r\n\r\n> Are there any rayon-like async runtimes that create a lot of worker threads to side step this issue? But so far the only things that I think are slowing the prompt down significantly are external commands and listing files in the current directory which should be easy to async. I guess git status is still slow and non-async until #1446 is fixed.\r\n\r\nI think [parallel-stream](https://docs.rs/parallel-stream/) looks interesting. The api is similar in concept to rayon, but it multiplexes futures across async runtime threads. There are a few issues with that particular crate however, but that direction looks promising, even if it might result in copying a small bit of its implementation into starship.\r\n\r\nAnd yes, git-status either needs to be switched to process invocation, or to a separate thread.\r\n\r\n> Regarding async I'm also a bit worried that the runtimes might have a high initial set-up cost before they get going.\r\n\r\nI don't think that setup overhead is a significant danger, since:\r\n1. rayon does have its own setup cost, which doesn't look meaningful today\r\n1. there are several async runtimes to choose from, so we can do A/B testing to find the most convenient. E.g. `smol` is a small and simple one.\r\n\r\nAll told, measurements are a must when deciding which runtime and parallelization strategy to pick.\r\n\r\n> You might be able to pick up some code from #922\r\n\r\nThanks for the link, I'll take a look! I'm currently doing a PoC to see which approach works most smoothly.\r\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
