    "body": "@davidkna \r\nAt least with my config on my machine, replacing rayon with async_std actually seems 1-2ms faster\r\n```bash\r\nstarship on  master [$?] is 📦 v0.48.0 via 🦀 v1.51.0-nightly took 6s \r\n23:49:45 ❯ hyperfine --warmup 10 -m 50 './async_bin prompt'\r\nBenchmark #1: ./async_bin prompt\r\n  Time (mean ± σ):     105.1 ms ±   4.3 ms    [User: 96.5 ms, System: 19.7 ms]\r\n  Range (min … max):    97.9 ms … 117.6 ms    50 runs\r\n \r\n\r\nstarship on  master [$?] is 📦 v0.48.0 via 🦀 v1.51.0-nightly took 6s \r\n23:50:22 ❯ hyperfine --warmup 10 -m 50 './sync_bin prompt'\r\nBenchmark #1: ./sync_bin prompt\r\n  Time (mean ± σ):     107.1 ms ±   6.5 ms    [User: 98.3 ms, System: 20.1 ms]\r\n  Range (min … max):    98.4 ms … 123.9 ms    50 runs\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis is a comparison between master at https://github.com/starship/starship/commit/a2058b791a894f3cdc369d198f537e08dd920fd8 , and https://github.com/alexmaco/starship/commit/6bfde592f32432551c9df529f50fa163fffcca12 , with the same degree of allowed parallelism, only now done in a style similar to the implementation of parallel-stream, and without adding any module-level timeout facilities. Feel free to double-check these measurements with other configs if you like.",
